   138	  decision); on the Mac the draft-5 tolerance criterion applies. It saves `gate-p0/gate.json` (with `identical`,
   139	  `require_identical` and `gate_ok`) and an inventory. On failure the driver stops before
   140	  any churn cell is dispatched and reports. Every later production stage, also when run alone or resumed, first requires
   141	  that saved gate record: passing its criterion (on the pod, bit-identical), for this cell and id mapping, and run by the
   142	  same driver version (its hash in the gate's inventory); production runs cannot skip the gate.
   143	
   144	## 4. Diagnostics (evaluation only, no search)
   145	
   146	For every cell, the base and the doubled winner are rerun on the evaluation seeds under churn (`diagnostics` stage):
   147	per-seed normalised profits; per episode the [served, audited] counts by identity age, in bins [0, 10), [10, 20),
   148	[20, 50), [50, 200), for honest replacement identities, honest initial identities, and the focal provider's identities;
   149	honest replacements per episode; and per episode two "newest" shares for the focal identity. (i) Creation-time newest:
   150	the share of rounds in which its creation round is strictly the latest among all current identities; this is not the
   151	order R3 uses. (ii) Mechanism-relevant: over the rounds in which it holds assigned jobs, the share in which its
   152	first-offer round is strictly the latest among the identities holding assigned jobs, which is how R3 ages and ranks
   153	identities (`sim/mechanisms/factorial.py`). Only (ii) is used to discuss R3's targeting.
   154	A bin with no served job is reported as unavailable. Pooling over markets: per rule, winner and bin, the mean of the
   155	market shares over the markets where the share is available, with that count. A higher audited share of honest
   156	newcomers is greater audit exposure; it is not by itself a financial loss.
   157	
   158	## 5. Analysis (fixed now)
   159	
   160	- Per market and pooled g_p(r), d_p, q_p, with paired seed bootstrap intervals (9,999 resamples, percentile, 95 percent;
   161	  the same resampled seed indices for every rule and rate of a market; pooled replicates resample seeds within each
   162	  market and average the six market means). The intervals condition on the six markets and the selected policies; they
   163	  do not include search uncertainty or uncertainty across markets.
   164	- Changes from no churn, paired by seed with the registered records: base with base, doubled with doubled.
   165	- Attack and reference profit terms separately; the reselection diagnostic per cell; sign counts over the six markets.
   166	- No confirmatory test, no multiplicity family, no change to any registered decision; all results are labelled
   167	  exploratory follow-up.
   168	
   169	## 6. Pre-specified expectations
   170	
   171	Written after the disclosed peek in section 1 and before any churn search. Scored on point estimates, as met (holds
   172	under both the base and the doubled searches, each against its own no-churn baseline), failed (fails under both), or not
   173	established (the two disagree).
   174	
   175	1. Pooled g_0.05(R3) exceeds the registered pooled no-churn g(R3) (base 0.0649; doubled against the doubled value).
   176	2. Pooled d_0.05 = C* - R3 is below the registered no-churn value (base 0.0207; doubled 0.0296).
   177	3. q_0.01 = B3 - R3 is positive on all six markets.
   178	4. At p = 0.05, the market-pooled audited share of honest replacement identities in their first ten rounds is higher
   179	   under R3 than under C* and than under B3, for both winners (a cross-rule difference under churn; no no-churn baseline
   180	   exists for honest replacement identities in these cells).
   181	
   182	A failure is not read as evidence of a particular cause. Only if expectation 2 is met may the paper say that R3's
   183	measured edge over C* shrinks when honest opponents churn; only if 4 is met that, under churn, R3 exposes honest newcomers
   184	to more audits than C* and B3 do.
   185	
   186	## 7. Cost and operations
   187	
   188	**Work** (`experiments/v2/benchmark/timings.json`, 0.108 USD per core-hour): per cell a D2+H3 search (2,084 s), the
   189	evaluation on 200 seeds (96.0 s per 160 seeds, scaled: 120 s), the doubling check with its held-out evaluation (998 s),
   190	and two diagnostic profiles (60 s); churn slows an episode by 1.133 (p = 0.01) and 1.330 (p = 0.05) (section 1). 36
   191	cells plus the gate cell: **41.1 core-hours, 4.43 USD** at the benchmark price.
   192	
   193	**Memory** (`docs/revision/churn/churn_memory.py`): one 200-seed profile peaks at 981 MB without churn and 1,084 MB at
   194	p = 0.05; an evaluate or doubling task keeps four profiles (2.5 GB without churn, operations ledger), so about 2.8 GB
   195	under churn. Workers: search 16; evaluate, doubling and diagnostics 9 (about 25 GB, 23.3 GiB, below the pod's 28 GiB floor; an
   196	independent four-profile check at p = 0.05 in review peaked at 2,532 MiB). **Pod-side preflight** before the evaluate and
   197	the doubling stage (`memory_preflight`): real tasks in child processes, one evaluate task per rule and one doubling task,
   198	each writing its production record (not run again); the largest peak times a margin of 1.25 for variation across
   199	markets, rules and winners; the cgroup limit and the parent's own peak; workers = min(current limit,
   200	floor((0.9 x limit - parent) / (1.25 x peak))), saved in `memory_preflight_<stage>.json`; the driver refuses if no task
   201	fits. **A task whose record already exists is no measurement** (review r4): the child reports the task status, and a
   202	"skipped" task is replaced by the next unfinished task of the same rule (evaluate) or stage (doubling). A retry reuses a
   203	saved measurement only if it applies (record version 2 with an explicit "done" status for every measured task, so
   204	draft-4 records are never reused; same memory limit and margin; measured jobs in the current job list; for the
   205	evaluation, a measurement for every current rule); with neither, the driver refuses. **A measurement never removes a
   206	job from dispatch** (review r5): every job of the stage passes through `_run_one`, which skips a valid record and
   207	recomputes a missing or damaged one; only the cost admission counts the tasks measured in the same command as done. **The chosen limit persists across commands:** it is saved in
   208	`memory_workers.json` with the memory limit, and every later command on that output (a separate stage, a resumed run)
   209	starts from the lowest saved limit that matches its memory limit; the diagnostics (two profiles per task, below the
   210	evaluate task) refuse to dispatch without such a saved limit. **Admission and the preflight:** before a stage the cost admission covers the preflight's serial tasks
   211	plus the remaining jobs at the current limit; after the preflight it runs again for the remaining jobs at the chosen
   212	limit, and stops between stages if the cap would be passed.
   213	
   214	**Billed pod time** (one 16-vCPU pod, 0.56 USD per hour; `docs/revision/churn/churn_cost.py`): the gate cell serially,
   215	then per rate the 18 searches in two waves of 16; the evaluation as three serial preflight tasks plus 15 jobs in two waves
   216	of 9; the doubling as one serial preflight task plus 17 jobs in two waves of 9; the diagnostics in two waves of 9; plus
   217	0.6 hours of preparation and idle: **6.88 hours, 3.85 USD** (5.95 hours and 3.33 USD without the serial preflight tasks).
   218	If the preflight cuts the evaluation workers to 2, the same arithmetic gives 12.44 hours, 6.97 USD, still below the
   219	stop cap; the admission after each preflight recomputes the stage cost at the chosen workers. The serial tasks add
   220	billed time, not core-hours, so the reservation below (4.43 USD unpadded, 5.54 USD reserved) still covers the expected
   221	3.85 USD.
   222	
   223	**Budget** (author, 2026-11-10). The arm uses the shared ledger category `followup` (cap 22 USD for all follow-up runs).
   224	Before the pod: `docs/revision/followup/budget_followup.py --reserve churn-pod 4.43` (the unpadded estimate; the
   225	wrapper reserves 4.43 x 1.25 = 5.54 USD). **Stop cap 8.00 USD, an estimate-based stage-admission limit.** No running
   226	stage is ever killed (author, 2026-11-10): before each stage the driver adds the pod time billed since allocation at the
   227	pod's actual hourly price, the charges of earlier failed attempts, and the stage's estimate, and stops between stages
   228	if the sum would pass the cap, then reports. Production runs refuse to start without `--started` (the allocation time)
   229	and `--usd-per-hour`; with `--prior-usd`, all are saved in `run.json`. A stage that overruns its estimate can end above
   230	the cap.
   231	
   232	**Launcher** (a copy of `results/pods/reset_pod.sh` with `podctl.py`, unchanged pod safety; draft
   233	`docs/revision/churn/churn_pod.sh`, installed in `results/pods/` after review): code and tarball guards; creation only
   234	before the hard deadline; the price checked against the ceiling `MAXCPH`; the memory floor (28 GiB); the gate, then the
   235	stages, each started only if no STOP file exists and the soft deadline has not passed (the soft deadline is the cap at
   236	the ceiling, net of earlier attempts' charges); at the soft deadline it writes STOP, so the pod stops after the running
   237	stage. **The one exception to "no running stage is killed":** the `podctl.py` watchdog deletes the pod at 1.3 times the
   238	cap at the price ceiling (10.40 USD at most for this arm), also mid-stage, as an absolute guard against a hung pod. Spend
   239	is recorded per attempt (pod id, start, end, recorded price) in the launcher's attempts file, which persists across
   240	launches; the summary gives the spend at the recorded prices and the bound at the ceiling. The launcher passes to the
   241	driver the current attempt's allocation time, the recorded price, and the earlier attempts' charges at their recorded
   242	prices. An EXIT, INT or TERM trap pulls the records, verifies them against the inventories, deletes the pod, and
   243	verifies the deletion; the watchdog keeps deleting until no pod of this name remains.
   244	
   245	## 8. Steps
   246	
   247	1. Internal review of this plan to consensus; freeze it (commit).
   248	2. The author's approval of the follow-up limit is recorded; the reservation of 5.54 USD under `followup`.
   249	3. One pod: the p = 0 gate, then the 36 cells; delete the pod and verify the deletion; record the spend.
   250	4. Analysis in `docs/revision/posthoc/` (tested first); a paper paragraph and table, labelled exploratory.
   251	
   252	## 9. Commands behind the numbers
   253	
   254	- Registered values: `python3 -c "import json;p=json.load(open('docs/revision/posthoc/posthoc.json'))['doubled']['D2+H3'];print(p['absolute'],p['pooled'])"`
   255	  (C* 0.0856, R3 0.0649; C* - R3 0.02072 base, 0.02962 doubled; B3 - R3 0.1734 base, 0.1698 doubled).
   256	- Timing and peek: `uv run python docs/revision/churn/churn_timing.py` (40 fresh0 evaluation seeds; 6.48, 7.34, 8.62 s).
   257	- Memory: `uv run python docs/revision/churn/churn_memory.py none` and `... 0.05` (981.0 and 1,084.4 MB).
   258	- Cost: benchmark arithmetic over `experiments/v2/benchmark/timings.json` with the measured slowdowns, the worker limits
   259	  above and two waves per stage (41.06 core-hours, 4.43 USD).
   260	- Billed time: `uv run python docs/revision/churn/churn_cost.py` (6.28 hours of stages plus 0.6, 6.88 billed hours,
   261	  3.85 USD; 5.95 hours, 3.33 USD without the serial preflight tasks; 12.44 hours, 6.97 USD at two evaluation workers).
   262	- Reselection-by-regeneration cost: `sim/manifest_v2.py` `menu_families` and `bank_rules(ctx, fam, 3, False)` over the six
   263	  markets and three rules: 60 D2+H3 and 60 X7-SC searches per rate, 12.19 USD per rate.
   264	- Tests: `uv run pytest -q docs/revision/churn` (32 passed, 146 s).

